<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Chebet measured a plot beside a road near Nyeri. Along a straight fence <m>{40}\,\text{m}</m> long, starting at one corner, she measured the distance (the offset) from the fence to the curved edge of the plot every <m>{10}\,\text{m}</m>.</p><tabular border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Distance along fence (m)</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{0}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{10}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{20}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{30}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{40}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Offset (m)</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{24}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{31}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{35}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{33}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{28}</m></cell></row></tabular><p>(a) How many strips do these measurements make?</p><div><fillin characters="4" name="ans1"/>  </div><p>(b) Use the trapezoidal rule to estimate the area of the plot.</p><div><fillin characters="8" name="ans2"/> <m>\text{m}^2</m>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) There are <m>{5}</m> offsets. The strips are the gaps between them, so there are <m>{4}</m> strips, each <m>h = {10}\,\text{m}</m> wide: <m>{40} \div {10} = {4}</m>.</p><p>(b) Each inner offset is a side of two trapezia, so it is counted twice. Multiply each offset by its weight:</p><tabular border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Offset (m)</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{24}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{31}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{35}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{33}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{28}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Weight</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{1}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{2}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{2}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{2}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{1}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Weight <m>\times</m> offset</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{24}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{62}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{70}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{66}</m></cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>{28}</m></cell></row></tabular><p><me>A \approx \tfrac{1}{2}h\left[y_0 + y_{ {4} } + 2(y_1 + \cdots + y_{ {3} })\right] = \tfrac{1}{2} \times {10} \times {250} = {1250}\,\text{m}^2</me></p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>4</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>1250</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>